"CS1651360","Salisbury NHS Foundation Trust","2 - High","17/09/2025 09:49:29","Closed","Application running very slow","12/02/2026 12:46:06","system","Acute OCM Support Level 2","Peter Moran","18/09/2025 13:40:41 - Peter Moran (Work notes)
On the Review server we have a AppPool Recycler, configured within D:\review\logs\Recycle logs, that is triggered every minute 24/7 to monitor the Review_AppPool and record the number of requests that are longer than 120s into a new daily log file within D:\review\logs\Recycle logs\. The daily counts for both August and September are of a similar order of magnitude with no significant increase since 15/09/2025 - see screenshots Recycle_log_Aug.png and Recycle_log_Sept.png These would be indicative of an increased workload for the web interface if there was a significant difference.

I've analysed long_queries.txt, which records the SQL Query that has taken longer than 10s to complete, within D:\review\ui and have noticed the following:

Counts of entries per day in date order for each week in September are:

Wk1 = 57, 129, 84, 87, 94, 12, 6
Wk2 = 52, 78, 83, 89, 133, 10, 20
Wk3 = 166, 2069, 3944, 768

As we can see there's been a significant increase since Monday 15th, first entry in Wk3.

18/09/2025 09:39:52 - Peter Moran (Work notes)
During a customer based meeting with Salisbury on Wed 17/09/2025 during the afternoon using EMIS MS Teams the customer shared the Review GUI screen it was noted that the application did load but was a little slow in response to GUI interactions and especially when performing extended date range searches.

A separate issue that was noted was when the user makes a secondary tQuest request from within the Review GUI this is also very slow to respond.

Action plan is to review the log files, especially the ""slow running queries"" log, and assess what could be causing them.
JJ mentioned that it is most likely due to Execution Plan being non-ideal - he has also supplied the SQL fix for slow report and request list screens from Review v1.13.0. It updates two stored procedures spGetPageOfResults and spGetPageOfRequests and alters the view patientName.

Check Secondary Care tQuest, TQUEST01WEB-P1, for the usual issues with older data and index fragmentation and statistics updates, etc.

CH was involved with an issue on Mon 15/09/2025, the first day that the new WinPathh_HL7 processing was used, and had noted the following:

The interface was erroring with the following two errors:

An issue processing request  has occurred: Index was outside the bounds of the array.

AND:

Could not find file 'E:\export\lims\'.

I think the second error is a red herring, and caused by the first error. Typically ""Index was outside the bounds of the array."" seems to mean that there are invalid non standard characters in something like the Clinical Details field.  The interface then looks like it goes 'BANG!' when it encounters something like this, and so just loops round.

Restarting the interface makes no difference, because its a specific request its getting stuck at, and until that request is sorted in some way, it won't go any further.
I did see that on their Internal tQuest on Monday, it occurred twice, but hadn't seen it again until now.

To get the rest of the requests out, I've had to mark in the database these specific requests as Failed.  Looking at the Clinical Details, they all seem to be when ordering Radiology and Pathology at the same time, and there are non standard characters somehow ending up in the clinical Details field (maybe users are copy/pasting from CRIS or something like that?).

An example of the clinical details is:

Comment on CRIS-CCHAPC-6899494-Protocol: CT Chest/Abdo/Pelvis ð With IV contrast  

ð is the non standard character, and we can't be certain, this is the issue causing it, but I know the interface doesn't like 'non standard' characters.

It looks like its when users are ordering Radiology, and then the Radiology EQS adds in a Urea, Electrolytes and Creatinine orderable (which the EQS automatically adds in if a result is not available for it within x timeframe).  The Radiology orderable goes via the RIS Interface, with the Urea and Electrolytes going over the LIMS Interface.

I think its worth the trust investigating what users do to enter these clinical details.  Do they copy/paste from another system, or is it voice dictation converting to words, and that's adding in this character? (A quick Google seems to suggest its used in Phonetic transcriptions, so maybe some kind of Voice to Text conversion takes place and its adding this character in)?

That's something they can do and see how that's ending up there.

Was also trying to work out whether they used to have the Urea and Electrolytes orderable was added automatically before from the EQS or did they order it separately.  That was to determine what the old logs was doing, e.g., did we send those characters across, or if they ordered it separately, did they write different clinical details, and therefore never been a problem before, or did the old interface cope with it, or maybe it never send clinical details before...

