17/11/2025 14:20:58 - Astha Agnihotri (Work notes)
Hi Alan,

Thank you for your query regarding files being held in the E:\archive\suspect\ folder. This happens when a previous processing action does not finish gracefully, often due to a system error or an unhandled exception. Keystone detects an issue and moves the files to the suspect directory as a precaution to prevent incomplete or problematic data from interfering with subsequent runs. This is by design and is logged with an event such as [General] Archived suspect file x as y.

There are several reasons why this can occur.
1.A fatal error during processing, sometimes reported as [General Fatal] Action finished due to error 'x'.
2.High memory usage at the time of processing, which can happen if one of the files is very large and exhausts available memory, causing the entire batch to fail.
3.Windows directory path length limits: if the combined path and filename exceed 245 characters, the process cannot complete and the files are archived as suspect. Long practice names combined with detailed filenames can easily exceed this limit.
4.File locking by other software such as antivirus: if the file is being accessed by another program, Keystone logs “access is denied,” and the file is moved to the suspect folder.

Suspect files do not appear under “Files Waiting” in the Monitor because they are archived outside the active processing queue. This is intentional to avoid reprocessing files that may have caused previous failures. If confirmed safe, these files can be manually reprocessed, as we did with the 66 files previously identified.

To help prevent this from happening again:

1.Review Keystone logs for any [General Fatal] errors or “access denied” messages, as these provide clues about the underlying cause.
2.Monitor system memory usage during peak times.
3.Keep practice names and file paths short to avoid exceeding Windows limits.
4.Exclude processing directories from antivirus real-time scanning.

Regards
Astha

